.TH FEHOLDEXCEPT 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
feholdexcept \- save floating-point environment and enter non-stop mode
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.PP
.BI "int feholdexcept(fenv_t *" envp );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR feholdexcept ()
function atomically performs the following three actions:
.IP 1. 4
Saves the current floating-point environment (rounding mode, exception sticky
flags, and exception trap masks) into the object pointed to by
.IR envp .
.IP 2. 4
Clears all floating-point exception sticky flags.
.IP 3. 4
Masks (disables traps for) all floating-point exceptions, entering
.I non-stop mode.
.PP
In non-stop mode, floating-point exceptions do not cause signals (SIGFPE);
instead, the exception sticky flags accumulate silently and can be inspected
afterward with
.BR fetestexcept (3).
.PP
The typical use of
.BR feholdexcept ()
is to isolate a block of floating-point code so that exceptions it raises do
not disturb the calling context.
After the block completes, call
.BR feupdateenv (3)
to restore the original environment and re-raise any exceptions that occurred
during the computation.
.PP
The value stored in
.IR *envp
is opaque and must only be passed to
.BR feupdateenv (3)
or
.BR fesetenv (3).
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR feholdexcept ()
returns zero on success.
A non-zero return value indicates that non-stop mode could not be established
(this is rare on conforming implementations).
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined.
The function does not set
.IR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example uses
.BR feholdexcept ()
and
.BR feupdateenv (3)
to perform a
.BR sqrt ()
call in an isolated floating-point environment.
Any exceptions raised inside the block are merged back into the caller's
environment by
.BR feupdateenv (3).
.PP
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
#include <fenv.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

double safe_sqrt(double x)
{
    fenv_t env;

    /* 1. Save environment, clear flags, mask all traps. */
    feholdexcept(&env);

    /* 2. Perform computation. Exceptions accumulate silently. */
    double result = sqrt(x);

    /* 3. Optionally inspect exceptions before restoring. */
    if (fetestexcept(FE_INVALID))
        printf("safe_sqrt: FE_INVALID raised (x=%g).\\n", x);

    /* 4. Restore original environment and re-raise new exceptions. */
    feupdateenv(&env);

    return result;
}

int main(void)
{
    double r = safe_sqrt(-1.0);
    printf("safe_sqrt(-1.0) = %g\\n", r);
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fenv (7),
.BR fegetenv (3),
.BR feupdateenv (3)
